  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon shows the persistent trough that \nhad been across the central CONUS continuing to weaken while a \nshortwave moving across Nebraska spurs on convection to its \nsoutheast in Kansas and Missouri. Dreary conditions have been \ngradually easing up with less and less surface observations coming \nin with lowered visibility. Rain chances for the rest of the day \nshould stay well to the northwest, with our best bet coming \nfrom an isolated shower or storm developing along a weak \nboundary in western NE/SD drifting into the area after 9 PM. \nOtherwise, it'll be a day to watch the edges of low cloud cover \nslowly erode while highs reach the lower 80s (or near 90 in \nnortheast Nebraska).\n\nShowers and storms return to the forecast Friday, manifesting in one \nof the two following scenarios: 1) Elevated storms develop along the \nSD/IA/MN border region that travel along the western Iowa border \nthrough 2 PM; or 2) Storms wait until 2-4 PM to develop along a \nfront before clearing the area to the south by 10 PM.\n\nFor scenario 1 (anchored by the HRRR), modest shear and\ninstability are set up along the nose of the low-level wind \nfields during the pre-dawn hours, with that nosing/impinging of \nthe front serving as the forcing. Once the storms get going, \nthey would be able to develop some semblance of a cold pool \nthat would bring 40+ mph gusts and some hail with them as they \npush southward along the border through the morning where a \nMUCAPE gradient will be set up. Scenario 2 seems to pose the \ngreater risk for severe weather (currently highlighted in a \nlevel 1 risk out of 5), where greater access to large-scale \ninstability and to the surface exists for more organized outflow\nto develop. This scenario would also have a wider affected area\ndue to the frontal placement being across south-central \nNebraska into central Iowa. A couple of the HREF members do \noutput gusts in excess of 50 kts (NAM Nest and FV3), with hail \nlarger than quarters also being possible. Storms should be \nprogressive enough to avoid any flooding concerns based on the \nlatest model output.\n\nSaturday and Beyond:\n\nDepending on what unfolds on Friday, another round or two of severe \nweather is on the menu for the forecast area Saturday. An \ningredients-based approach to the forecast draws the attention \nquickly to a reservoir of instability arcing from northwest Nebraska \ndown to the south-central portion of the state near the Kansas \nborder region. A mid-level shortwave with slightly enhanced mid-\nupper flow will move across the area, providing enhanced shear to go \nalong with sufficiently steep mid-level lapse rates for severe-\nintensity storms, it'll just be a matter of when they develop. \nSignificant spread continues to exist with the development of the \nsurface fields temperature-wise Saturday, but regardless a gradient \nin the surface fields will develop and strengthen over the course of \nthe day. Either convergence along that gradient or the \naforementioned shortwave will be the catalyst for storm development, \nwith that activity working with the northwesterly steering flow to \nmove across the area. Early peeks at the extended CAMs indicate \nelevated storms may be able to develop as early as 7 AM, while any \ndelay in activity into the later morning/afternoon hours would \nincrease the overall ceiling for any MCS that would occur. With \ndeeper-shear vectors aligning with the instability gradient, a \nhealthier and prolonged wind threat seems possible. Highs in tho\nupper 80s to low 90s are on tap, with the caveat being that \nstorms could some variance based on the time of day they move \nthrough. \n\nBeyond Saturday we continue to see ourselves on the northeastern \nedge of the broader ridging pattern centered to the west. We'll see \nhighs trend warmer, but still relatively close to normal for this \ntime of year. A persistent frontal zone will be oriented near to \njust northwest of the area, keeping rain and a stray strong storm or \ntwo a low-end possibility.",
